<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Turkmen SSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1924 CE – 1991 CE · capital Ashgabat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Soviet republic of the Turkmen, a desert land of gas fields and cotton built around the Karakum Canal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> SSR was created in October 1924 in the national delimitation of Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, from the Transcaspian region of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turkestan-assr">Turkestan ASSR</a> and Turkmen districts of the Bukharan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khorezm</a> republics. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashgabat">Poltoratsk</a>, renamed Ashgabat in 1927.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Soviet rule settled nomadic and semi-nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes, collectivised farming and fought Basmachi resistance led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junaid-khan">Junaid Khan</a> into the 1930s. In 1948 an earthquake destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashgabat">Ashgabat</a>, killing tens of thousands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakum-canal">Karakum Canal</a>, begun in the 1950s, carried water from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and natural gas became a major export. Turkmen was written in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet from 1928 and in Cyrillic from 1940.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saparmurat-niyazov">Saparmurat Niyazov</a>, first secretary from 1985, led the republic to independence on 27 October 1991, when it became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/turkmen-ssr.jpg" alt="Turkmen SSR" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Denelson83, Urmas, Nokka · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Turkmen_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1974%E2%80%931991),_Flag_of_Turkmenistan_(1991%E2%80%931992).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1924 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ashgabat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Saparmurat Niyazov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (state atheism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkmen, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Turkmen Soviet Socialist Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1958&amp;focus=turkmen-ssr" aria-label="Turkmen SSR on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6135 813 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Turkmen SSR lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6455 1069L6691 960M6455 1069L6473 958M6455 1069L5820 533M6455 1069L6455 1069"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6691 960h0M6473 958h0M5820 533h0M6455 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6455 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6455 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ashgabat. <a href="/atlas?year=1958&amp;focus=turkmen-ssr">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
